From 656f1b0b5b1594c493595a778fd8706a084e0b99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: chrg6 <chrg6@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:57:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the fraction of a frame the ocarina recorder's clock was
 losing above 60 fps, in a new patch set orecord.

The ocarina manager counts the length of each note and rest in whole 60 Hz frames and adds the time manager's frames
per update to that clock at four sites (main+0x32926c8, main+0x3293410, main+0x3296b08, main+0x3296da4) through
fcvtzu, which cuts the step toward zero. Above 60 fps the step is under one frame, so the clock never moved: a
recording kept its notes without their lengths (the recorder leaves a length of 0 out), the 360 and 120 frame limits
never came, and a recording ended after a fixed 60 frames of its timer instead of following the clock; at the 40 and
45 fps limits it counted one a frame. The set replaces each fcvtzu with nine words that add the fraction carried on
the patch page's +264, store what is left after the cut (NaN and negatives as 0) and convert the sum. Steps of one
and two frames give the game's count with the fraction left at 0, so 30 and 60 fps are unchanged. The words call
nothing and take no lock (one of the functions returns through an unsaved x30), and use only v16-v18, which the three
functions never name. The set is appended so that no existing set's index moves; ocarina_timing = 0 gives the game's
words.

Not reproduced before the change (read from the code). The generator's check of the original words passed against
the 1.1.1 main, the new tests/ocarina_timing_check.py passed (.venv, Unicorn), as did frame_step_check,
text_speed_check, sprint_check, movie_timeline_check, cfg_parse_check, launcher_test and cfg_rows_check.

(cherry picked from upstream 5f9374e)
